WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.531
Imagine adding up a long shopping receipt twice and getting the same total. You know the adding is right. But if the shop charged you for the wrong item, the total would still add up. A trial balance works the same way.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:28.946
A trial balance is a list of every ledger balance on one date, with debits in one column and credits in the other. It is a list, not an account, so it is not part of the double entry.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:45.198
So what does it do? It tests the arithmetic of the double entry, gathers the balances for the financial statements, and warns you when totals differ. But some errors leave the totals equal, such as omission, commission and principle.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:59.208
First, read the command word and the marks. State wants short points. Explain wants a point and a reason. Evaluate wants both sides and a judgement.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:11.441
For a benefit, make the point, then develop it. A trial balance checks the arithmetic of the double entry, because every debit has an equal credit, so the two totals must agree.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:23.112
For a limitation, say that agreeing totals do not prove the books are correct, and name an error that stays hidden. Then finish with a clear judgement, using the trader's name.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:39.190
Here is a real question from June 2021. Jacques prepares a trial balance. We must evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of preparing one. Five marks.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:53.004
Earlier in the question, Jacques found errors. Error one: motor insurance was posted to the motor vehicles account. An expense went into an asset account. That is an error of principle.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:03.314
The advantage. A trial balance checks the arithmetic of Jacques's double entry, because the total of the debit balances should equal the total of the credit balances.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:16.469
The disadvantage. Totals that agree do not prove the books are correct. The motor insurance was still debited and credited with the same amount, so the totals agree, but the error is hidden.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:32.854
Finally, the judgement. On balance, Jacques should keep preparing a trial balance. It is a quick, useful check before he prepares his financial statements, even though it will not catch every error. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:44.283
A common mistake is saying that a balanced trial balance proves there are no errors. It only shows that the debits equal the credits.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:51.997
Another is writing only advantages, with no conclusion. Evaluate needs both sides, then a judgement.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:03:02.606
Now you try. Read what Priya's assistant says, then answer both parts. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:21.067
For part a: it checks the arithmetic of the double entry. For part b: agreeing totals do not prove the books are correct, because some errors do not affect the totals. For example, if a sale was left out completely, both the debit and the credit are missing.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:29.797
Remember: a benefit with its reason, a limitation with a named error, and a clear judgement. Now try the practice questions.
